I think there is no need to "organize" it in a special way. Just give
the people ordinary accounts...

> To prevent misuse, and to be able to control what happens, we would

To control what happens? I.e. to mess with your users' privacy? Not
exactly a good idea, I think.

> a. How do universities that give network access to large groups of
>    students organize this, and how do they prevent misuse on the
>    software, hardware, and organizational level.

At our University, students can get accounts with full network access
on a 3090 (MVS/ESA) and on a HP (HPUX). Both of these systems provide
full access to all of the system's facilities without embedding it
into special shells etc.

The only restrictions on the use of these machines are:
- On the 3090, which is shared with the rest of the University and has a
facility for accounting of CPU time, the CPU time one can use is
restricted. This holds not only for students and is generally not
considered a problem.
- On the HP, access to network servies is restricted by a packet
filter for reasons of "preventing misuse". This is a major headache
when using the system since every other legitimate system facility is
disrupted too, and nobody can tell so far if it is effective in
preventing misuse (the system is rather new) - but I doubt there would
be much problems without the restrictions. (In fact, there would
likely be less problems.)

During the past few years, we had no incidents of misuse. Which proves
again that students can be much more reasonable people than common
belief holds. :-)

> b. Are there any Unix "bulletin board" like systems that function
>    like a kind of shell for network / mail / news access for large
>    groups of students, and do you have any experience with these
>    systems (how managable is it).

I don't have experiences with such systems, but IMHO you'd do best
with abandoning that idea and giving the users shells, based on the
following premises:
- Effectively *preventing* any kind of misuse is impossible.
- Of a large population of students, one or two may actually do
something wrong, but you have to deal with them case by case anyway,
and dealing with all as if they were possible intruders will only piss
off the 99+% innocent users.

