Here are a few experiences that might help you plan strategy:

CASE 1. NCSA at U. of Illinois:

 The problem (from CAF News):
   "4. [Editor's note: This policy was recently changed -cmk] The NCSA
   email policy permits searches and punishment of facility users who
   criticize the NCSA or University in email. The policy was created
   to justify (after the fact) a search of the computer files of a
   student employee." The policy was based on what business do;
   neglecting academic freedom and that students are customers not
   employees.
       (see ftp.eff.org:pub/academic/abstracts)

 The solution:
   Via articles CAF-Talk and uiuc.general and email, we were able to
   get the interest of an grad college admin and a campus-wide
   chancellor's computer committee. They created a new campus-wide
   policy that overruled the NCSA policy. We were also talking to a
   University senate committee as a back up.
     (The new policy is ftp.eff.org:pub/academic/policies/uiuc.edu)


CASE II. Proposed U. of Illinois "verbal abuse" policy

  The problem: Would have made much offensive speech subject to
   university sanction. The proposal came from a Senate committee; all
   the comittee members seem to think (probably still do) that the
   proposal was a pretty neat idea.

  The solution (that I only had a little hand in):
    The school paper ran articles. Senators not on the comitttee
    expressed concern. (I sent email to every senator I could).  In a
    debate before the *full* senate, the proposal was blasted and sent
    back to committee.


Possible lessons:

   If you can't persuade the committee try to go over their head by
    persuading members of the full Senate. Try to find allies in the
    Senate. The newspaper can be an effective way inform.

Good luck.

- Carl
