   pursuit of truth, the development of students, and the general
   well-being of society. Free inquiry and free expression are
   indispensable to the attainment of these goals. As members of the
   academic community, students should be encouraged to develop the
   capacity for critical judgment and to engage in a sustained and
   independent search for truth."
     - The beginning of "Joint Statement on Rights and Freedoms of Students",
       the main U.S. statement of academic freedom for students.

A university that wants to fulfill its academic mission should
encourage student-directed use of computer resources. To restrict
computer use to classroom problems is morally equivalent to forbidding
students from exploring their library on their own.


With regard to requiring ethical use:

This requirement is circular. The purpose of this policy is to detail
what ethical use is required.


With regard to politeness:

    "All persons in canada have the fundamental right, as embodied in the
    nation's Bill of Rights and the Canadian Charter of Rights and Freedoms, to
    have access to all expressions of knowledge, creativity and intellectual
    activity, and to express their thoughts publicly.  This right to
    intellectual freedom, under the law, is essential to the health and
    development of Canadian society."
       -- Canadian Library Associations Statement on Intellectual Freedom


   "Freedom of thought and expression is essential to any institution of
    higher learning. Universities and colleges exist not only to transmit
    existing knowledge.  Equally, they interpret, explore, and expand that
    knowledge by testing the old and proposing the new.

    This mission guides learning outside the classroom quite as much as in
    class, and often inspires vigorous debate on those social, economic,
    and political issues that arouse the strongest passions. In the
    process, views will be expressed that may seem to many wrong,
    distasteful, or offensive. Such is the nature of freedom to sift and
    winnow ideas."
      -- "On Freedom of Expression and Campus Speech Codes" by
                      the American Association of University Professors.


On a treating computers different than other university media and resources:

   "Principle: The principles of academic freedom apply to academic
    computer systems. Computer polices should be consistent with general
    university codes and widely accepted statements on academic freedom
    such as the Joint Statement on Rights and Freedoms of Students."
      -- From the draft, unofficial
               "Statement on Computers and Academic Freedom"

      "Resolved: That the same standards and principles of
    intellectual and academic freedom developed for university
    libraries be applied to material received from the news network. [...]
       Resolved: That the same standards of intellectual and academic
    freedom developed for faculty and student publication in
    traditional media be applied to publication in computer media."
       --  Policy of the U. of Wisconsin-Milwaukee.

>2-	Use only those facilities which are in the public domain or for which 
>	they have obtained explicit authorization, at McGill or at any other
>	location accessible through a network.

This is very poorly worded. In the U.S. and I believe in Canada, the
phrase "public domain" refers to expressive material, not facilities.

>3-	Take all necessary steps to protect the integrity of MCF. In parti-
>	cular, the users shall not share with others the access codes, account
>	numbers, passwords or other authorization which have been assigned 
>	them.

The phrase "necessary" should be replaced with "reasonable" (unless
the policy intends to authorize the use of deadly force to protect
passwords).

>4-	Respect the copyrights of owners of all software and data they use.

This is not grammatical. I suggest "Respect copyrights."

[...]

>Users should recognize that authorized McGill personnel may have access to 
>data and software stored in MCF while performing routine operations or 
