I personally believe that no single source of input in today's complex
world is sufficient for the political decision makers to make an informed
decision.  All communications channels should be exploited to
the maximum extent possible.  The alternative is to deny communication
via one or more modes, thus denying the convenience or expressive
capacity of those prohibited modes.  The extreme case of this is to deny
the credibility of all modes and merely rely upon the supposed
superior intellect of the decision maker to fathom the mood of
his or her constituency from an isolated ivory tower.  That is surely
not what Mr. Gill or any other staffer would advocate, I'm certain.
 
So, when assessing the potential of *any* means of communications, the
White House ought to look at the other forms in current use and
determine why the new means should be prohibited, not why it should
be allowed; how it should be interpreted, not how might be abused.
This is the ultimate openness which a democratic society demands of
its leaders.
 
I am willing to trust my elected officials to
consider the source when reviewing electronic input (via a product, any product
like eVote in effect) just as I trust them to consider the source when
reviewing the input thru the White House switchboard or from snail mail.
