mus equally likely in the others.  It would be ludicrous to advocate
closing down the call in service or the postal service to the White
House.  E-mail and collection of electronic input
should be no different.  It does not demand any greater immediacy from
the decision maker, it merely affords the sender greater immediacy.  It
carries equivalent flaws (from a political theorywpoint) as other
sources of input in that it is biased towards a segment of the citizenry which h
as access to the vehicle of communication.  This is no greater and
no lesser, though, than the bias inherent in the telephone or the postal
service.  It is certainly no more biased than formal political polls
such as are performed by the polling industry (Gallup et al).  All meds
of collecting such information on the concerns and will of 200,000,000
people will fall short in some way; all must be assessed on their strenghths
as well as their weaknesses

At least this is my humble opinion as one of the electorate who casts
his vote for political decision makers upon whom I must then place my
confidence and trust to use any and all means to keep in touch with
the will of the people.
 
