At the beginning of this semester at the University of Tennessee,
Knoxville, a good friend of mine went to pick up his schedule, just to find
out that there was a student conduct case pending against him. After
investigation he found out that he was accused of taking a stack of about
10 (in words: ten) freely distributed newspapers and put them into a
recycling bin. He was supposedly filmed by a security camera doing so. The
newspaper was "The Conservative Spectator" the semi-official organ of UT's
college republicans. The 'Spectator' had been offering a reward to anyone
providing them with evidence that their paper was being stolen and/or
destroyed. 

Well, the editor of the paper insisted that the person on the tape was my
friend and there was a (alas very brief) hearing. After the other witness
saw my friend they changed their opinion and the case was dismissed.
Needles to say that the person on the tape didn't even look like my friend
in the slightest. Our best guess is that the case was used so as a test balloon from the editor's
side to see if UT would actually prosecute someone for taking their free
paper, (Note: my friend is an active member in the Young/College
Democrats), and then whine about it if UT doesn't. Don't forget we're not
talking about hundreds of papers but a couple. 

One interesting fact: My friend was not charged with infringing on anyone's
freedom of speech but rather with theft and vandalism (coming back to the
question if you can steal anything that's free). Needless to say that all
my friend got was a 3-line apology in the "Spectator." 

I agree that infringing on someone's freedom of speech is a student conduct
case, but I hated the way the "Spectator" abused an innocent bystander for
a show "trial"...

