From: kadie@eff.org (Carl M. Kadie)
Newsgroups: comp.org.eff.talk,alt.comp.acad-freedom.talk,alt.privacy
Subject: The CERT policy "virus" spreads (was Big Brother is watching!!)
Date: 7 Nov 1993 12:24:00 -0500
Message-ID: <2bjavg$29a@eff.org>

The rules against unathorized access at U. of Wisconsin at Eau Claire
and Salt Lake Community College seem to eliminate all privacy
protection for *authorized* users suspected of rule violations.

Who can we thank?

Well here is part of the Utah rules:

> This system is a for the use of AUTHORIZED USERS ONLY.  Individuals using 
> computer system without authority, or in excess of their authority, are
> subject to having all of their activities on this system monitored and
> recorded by system personnel.

And here part of the CERT policy recommendation:

       This system is for the use of authorized users only.
       Individuals using this computer system without authority, or in
       excess of their authority, are subject to having all of their
       activities on this system monitored and recorded by system
       personnel. (see ref).

So CERT, an organization changed with protecting user privacy, is
likely the cause of one of the largest threats to user privacy on the
Net, a threat to the fourth amendment rights of every authorized
computer user at a state university.

Now you might say that CERT isn't responsible, that its recommendation
is being misapplied and that it didn't intend for it to be spread to
so many systems. But isn't that just the "Morris" defense? It didn't
work for Morris; it shouldn't work for CERT.

CERT should retract is recommendation and replace it with one that
respects authorized users' constitutional "right to be secure in their
persons, houses, papers, and effects, against unreasonable searches
and seizures".

- Carl Kadie

